  <p>This calculator finds a right triangle's hypotenuse by adding its two projection segments.</p>
  <p>Projections p and q are the two pieces of the hypotenuse created when the altitude from the right angle meets it. Enter both lengths, and the calculator adds them with the formula <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} /> to return the full hypotenuse. For example, if <span set:html={rich('[[p = 4]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9]]')} /> cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[c = 4 + 9 = 13]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the longest side of the right triangle and sits opposite the right angle. The calculator appears first on this page, followed by the formula, the geometry behind it, worked examples, and ways to verify your answer.</p>
  <p>The guide suits middle school students meeting projections for the first time, high school geometry classes, and anyone checking homework. Every formula is worked with real numbers, so you can follow each step without pausing to decode symbols.</p>
</section>

