<!-- Section 3: sec-what -->
<section id="sec-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Are Hypotenuse Projections in a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Start with a right triangle and draw the altitude from the right-angle vertex perpendicular to the hypotenuse. The altitude touches the hypotenuse at one point, called the foot, and that point splits the hypotenuse into two adjacent segments. Those two segments are the hypotenuse projections: <span set:html={rich('[[p = AD]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q = DB]]')} /> when the triangle is ABC with its right angle at C and altitude CD. The <a href="/right-triangle-projection-calculator/">projection calculator</a> treats each segment separately.</p>
  <p>A projection is not a leg. The legs a and b are the two sides that form the right angle, while the projections p and q lie flat along the hypotenuse itself. Mixing these up is the most common labeling error, so always ask: is this length on the hypotenuse, or is it one of the sides around the right angle?</p>
  <p>Here is a beginner-friendly example. If the altitude divides a hypotenuse into a 4 cm segment and a 9 cm segment, then <span set:html={rich('[[p = 4]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9]]')} /> cm, and the full hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 4 + 9 = 13]]')} /> cm. The formula <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} /> simply rebuilds the whole side from its two parts.</p>
  <p>A quick labeling habit prevents most errors: start at the right-angle vertex C, draw the altitude to the hypotenuse, and mark the foot D. The segment touching A is p and the segment touching B is q. Once those two labels are set, the rest of the guide applies directly.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-understanding -->
<section id="sec-understanding" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Hypotenuse and Its Two Segments</h2>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, and in any nondegenerate right triangle it is the longest side. When the altitude from the right-angle vertex drops perpendicular to it, the hypotenuse is divided into two shorter segments that sit end to end on the same straight line.</p>
  <p>Because both segments lie on that one side, they behave like two adjacent pieces of a ruler: placing them together rebuilds the whole side exactly, with no overlap and no gap. That is why the projections always add up to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>These labels match the main diagram below. Vertices A and B are the acute-angle corners, C is the right-angle vertex, and D is the foot of altitude CD on hypotenuse AB, with <span set:html={rich('[[AD = p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[DB = q]]')} />.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
