</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Hypotenuse From Projections Formula</h2>
  <p>The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} />, where c is the complete hypotenuse, p is the first projection segment, and q is the second. It uses addition rather than multiplication or square roots because the two projections are adjacent parts of one straight side, and segment addition rebuilds that side from its parts. Think of measuring two pieces of one board and adding the measurements to get the board's full length.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = p + q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>A frequent question is why the formula does not average the two segments. Averaging would give half the hypotenuse, which answers a different question. Addition rebuilds the whole side; the average and the geometric mean belong to other formulas covered later in this guide.</p>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[p = 7]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 11]]')} /> cm. Substitution gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7 + 11]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[c = 18]]')} /> cm. The formula itself is simple; the important part is understanding which two segments count as projections.</p>
  <p>Both segments must use compatible units before you add them. If p is in millimeters and q is in centimeters, convert one of them first, exactly as this page's calculator prompts you to do.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-how -->
<section id="sec-how" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Hypotenuse From Projections Calculator</h2>
  <p>Nine short steps take you from a labeled diagram to a verified hypotenuse.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the right triangle in your problem and confirm it has a right angle.</li>
    <li>Locate the hypotenuse: the side opposite the right angle.</li>
    <li>Find the point where the altitude from the right angle meets the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Identify the two projections p and q as the segments on either side of that point, using the <a href="/right-triangle-projection-p-calculator/">projection p calculator</a> guide for the first segment. The <a href="/right-triangle-projection-q-calculator/">projection q calculator</a> covers the second segment in the same way.</li>
    <li>Convert both measurements to compatible units if needed.</li>
    <li>Enter p in the Projection p field and q in the Projection q field.</li>
    <li>Click the Calculate Hypotenuse c button.</li>
    <li>Read the hypotenuse result in your chosen unit.</li>
    <li>Use Reset before starting a new problem.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Correct identification matters most. The fields expect the two segments of the hypotenuse itself, not the legs and not the altitude. Entering the wrong pair still produces a sum, but it will not be the hypotenuse.</p>
