<!-- Section 7: sec-manual -->
<section id="sec-manual" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Hypotenuse From Projections Manually</h2>
  <p>Manual calculation is a single substitution into <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} />. Take <span set:html={rich('[[p = 3.6]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.4]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = p + q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substitute the values: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 3.6 + 6.4]]')} />. Add them: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Line up the decimal points when adding by hand: 3.6 plus 6.4 gives 10.0, and the trailing zero can be dropped for a clean <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm. Keep the unit attached to the answer; a bare number is not a complete length.</p>
  <p>No Pythagorean calculation is needed, because both projection lengths are already known. To double-check, confirm the sum makes sense: 3.6 plus 6.4 is clearly 10, and the hypotenuse must be longer than either projection alone.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-diagram -->
<section id="sec-diagram" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Hypotenuse Projection Diagram Explained</h2>
  <p>The main diagram shows triangle ABC with the right angle at C. Side AC is leg a, side BC is leg b, and side AB is the hypotenuse c. Altitude CD drops from C perpendicular to AB and touches it at D.</p>
  <p>A good reading order starts at vertex C: trace leg a down to A, leg b across to B, then run along hypotenuse c from A to B. Pause at D, the foot of the altitude, to see the split into p and q. Reading the diagram this way wires the labels to the formula.</p>
  <p>Point D splits AB into two parts: segment AD is projection p and segment DB is projection q. The labels sit directly on the diagram so you can see where p begins, where q ends, and how the two together span the whole side from A to B.</p>
  <p>The right-angle markers at C and D confirm the geometry. The marker at C makes AB the hypotenuse, and the marker at D confirms that CD is the altitude. The original triangle is now divided into two smaller right triangles, ACD and CBD, which return later in this guide.</p>
