<!-- Section 9: sec-why -->
<section id="sec-why" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Hypotenuse Equal the Sum of Its Projections?</h2>
  <p>The reason is the segment addition postulate: if point D lies on segment AB between A and B, then the length AB equals AD plus DB. Here D is the foot of the altitude and A and B are the hypotenuse endpoints, so <span set:html={rich('[[AB = AD + DB]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = p + q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Substituting the standard symbols gives the formula directly. In a nondegenerate right triangle the altitude's foot always lands between the endpoints, so the postulate always applies. No trigonometry is needed for this step.</p>
  <p>Triangle similarity is the tool behind the deeper projection relationships, such as <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = cp]]')} />, but simple segment addition is enough to establish <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} />. The diagram below shows the same idea on a single straight line.</p>
  <p>One detail deserves emphasis: D always falls strictly between A and B in a genuine right triangle, never at an endpoint and never outside the segment. That placement is what makes the segment addition postulate applicable, and it is why zero or negative projections are rejected.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('hypotenuse-segment-addition.svg')} alt="Straight segment AB divided at point D into segment AD labeled p and segment DB labeled q, showing the whole segment equals p plus q" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A point between A and B splits the segment: AB = AD + DB.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Hypotenuse From Projections</h2>
  <p>Each example states the given values, the substitution, and the final answer. The commentary stays short so the arithmetic stays visible.</p>
  <p>All examples keep units consistent from the given values to the answer. When units differ, the example converts first and says so explicitly, so you can copy the habit into your own work.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Whole Numbers</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 4]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9]]')} /> cm, the formula <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = 4 + 9]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13]]')} /> cm. Two whole-number projections produce a whole-number hypotenuse here, though that is not guaranteed in general.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Equal Projections</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 5]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5 + 5 = 10]]')} /> cm. Equal projections mean the altitude strikes the midpoint of the hypotenuse, which happens exactly when the two legs are equal: an isosceles right triangle. Because the legs are equal, the altitude is also the median, which is why it bisects the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Decimal Projections</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 3.6]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.4]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 3.6 + 6.4 = 10]]')} /> m. Line up the decimal points and add as usual; the unit stays meters throughout.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Unequal Projections</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 2]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 18]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2 + 18 = 20]]')} /> cm. The projections do not need to be close in size; the sum still rebuilds the full hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Larger Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 125]]')} /> mm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 275]]')} /> mm, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 125 + 275 = 400]]')} /> mm. Large values change nothing about the method.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Mixed Units</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 40]]')} /> mm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9]]')} /> cm, convert first: 40 mm equals 4 cm. Then <span set:html={rich('[[c = 4 + 9 = 13]]')} /> cm. Adding before converting would give a meaningless 49.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Fractional Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = \\frac{1}{2}]]')} /> ft and <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{3}{4}]]')} /> ft, write both with denominator 4: <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\frac{2}{4} + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{5}{4}]]')} /> ft, which is 1.25 ft.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Finding a Missing Projection</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm, solve <span set:html={rich('[[q = c - p]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[q = 25 - 9 = 16]]')} /> cm. This inverse step shows the relationship runs both ways, though the calculator on this page adds p and q to find c rather than solving for a missing projection.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-legs -->
<section id="sec-legs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Hypotenuse Projections and Triangle Legs</h2>
  <p>The projections also determine the legs through the projection theorem: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = cp]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = cq]]')} />. Each leg is the geometric mean of the hypotenuse and its adjacent projection, a fact that follows from the similarity of the smaller triangles to the original.</p>
  <p>Taking square roots gives <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{cp}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{cq}]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm, first find <span set:html={rich('[[c = 9 + 16 = 25]]')} /> cm. Then <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{25 \\times 9} = \\sqrt{225} = 15]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{25 \\times 16} = \\sqrt{400} = 20]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse is therefore the key that unlocks the remaining sides: once c is known, each leg follows from its own projection. The <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">side calculator</a> solves similar missing-side problems directly.</p>
  <p>The pairing is not arbitrary: leg a sits against projection p in the small triangle ACD, which is similar to the whole triangle, so a relates to c and p while b relates to c and q. Remembering which leg pairs with which projection keeps the square roots on the correct segments.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('hypotenuse-projection-theorem-legs.svg')} alt="Right triangle with projections 9 and 16 and hypotenuse 25, showing leg a equals 15 from a squared equals 25 times 9, and leg b equals 20 from b squared equals 25 times 16" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Leg a = sqrt(25 x 9) = 15; leg b = sqrt(25 x 16) = 20.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-altitude -->
<section id="sec-altitude" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Hypotenuse Projections Relate to Altitude</h2>
  <p>The same projections yield the altitude through the geometric mean theorem: <span set:html={rich('[[h^2 = pq]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{pq}]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{9 \\times 16} = \\sqrt{144} = 12]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Compare the two formulas: <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} /> adds the projections, while <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{pq}]]')} /> takes their geometric mean. They answer different questions. The hypotenuse is the full side length; the altitude is the perpendicular height from the right angle down to that side.</p>
  <p>For altitude problems, use the <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-from-projections-calculator/">altitude-from-projections calculator</a>, which is built around <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{pq}]]')} />. Keep the two results distinct: in this triangle the hypotenuse is 25 cm while the altitude is only 12 cm.</p>
  <p>A built-in sanity check applies here: the altitude is always shorter than the hypotenuse. In the worked example, 12 cm is well under 25 cm. If your altitude ever exceeds your hypotenuse, recheck which formula you used.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('hypotenuse-altitude-p9q16.svg')} alt="Right triangle with projections 9 and 16 on the hypotenuse, hypotenuse 25, and altitude 12 drawn perpendicular to the hypotenuse" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Same projections, two formulas: c = 9 + 16 = 25, h = sqrt(9 x 16) = 12.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-pythagoras -->
<section id="sec-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Hypotenuse Projections and the Pythagorean Theorem</h2>
  <p>The projection relationships agree exactly with the Pythagorean theorem. Start from the projection theorem: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = cp]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = cq]]')} />. Adding the two equations gives <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = cp + cq]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c(p + q)]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Factoring out c is valid because it multiplies both terms. Since <span set:html={rich('[[p + q = c]]')} />, substitution yields:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>A numerical check with the 9-16-25 triangle confirms the algebra: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = 225 + 400 = 625]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c^2 = 25^2 = 625]]')} />. Both sides agree, which is exactly what the factored proof predicts.</p>
  <p>The proof closes neatly: the simple segment sum sits at the heart of the Pythagorean relationship. The diagram below shows where <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = cp]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = cq]]')} /> come from: each small triangle shares its angles with the original, so corresponding sides stay proportional.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('hypotenuse-similar-triangles.svg')} alt="Original right triangle ABC beside the two smaller similar triangles ACD and CBD with matching angles and corresponding sides labeled" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangles ACD and CBD are similar to ABC, giving a squared equals cp and b squared equals cq.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify Your Hypotenuse Calculation</h2>
  <p>Three quick checks catch almost every calculation error. The first is the direct computation; the other two confirm it from different angles.</p>
  <h3>Check 1: Recompute the Segment Addition</h3>
  <p>Add p and q again, slowly. With <span set:html={rich('[[p = 4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9]]')} />, confirm <span set:html={rich('[[4 + 9 = 13]]')} />. A careless slip here is the most common source of a wrong answer.</p>
  <h3>Check 2: Confirm the Legs Fit Pythagoras</h3>
  <p>Compute the legs from the projections, <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{cp}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{cq}]]')} />, then verify <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 20]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[225 + 400 = 625]]')} />. This geometric check goes beyond the calculator's addition.</p>
  <h3>Check 3: Sanity-Check the Diagram</h3>
  <p>Confirm that both projections are positive, that both lie on the same hypotenuse, and that c is longer than either segment alone. If the result is smaller than p or q, a value was entered in the wrong field.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-special -->
<section id="sec-special" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Special Cases and Important Properties</h2>
  <p>Every property below follows directly from addition, which makes them easy to trust.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Equal projections: if <span set:html={rich('[[p = q]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2p]]')} />. The altitude lands at the midpoint, and the two legs are equal, giving an isosceles right triangle.</li>
    <li>Unequal projections: any positive pair works. The altitude leans toward the longer projection, and the nearer leg is the shorter one.</li>
    <li>Very small projections: the formula still holds, for example <span set:html={rich('[[p = 0.5]]')} /> mm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 1.5]]')} /> mm give <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2]]')} /> mm.</li>
    <li>Very large projections: there is no upper limit, for example <span set:html={rich('[[p = 1200]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 800]]')} /> m give <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2000]]')} /> m.</li>
    <li>Scaling: doubling both projections doubles the hypotenuse, since <span set:html={rich('[[2p + 2q = 2(p + q)]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Swapping p and q: <span set:html={rich('[[p + q = q + p]]')} />, so the hypotenuse is unchanged and only the labels AD and DB trade places.</li>
    <li>Zero-length projections: <span set:html={rich('[[p = 0]]')} /> means the altitude coincides with a leg, so the triangle is degenerate and the calculator does not accept it.</li>
    <li>Negative projections: lengths cannot be negative, so a negative entry is always a data error.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The diagram compares two triangles with the same hypotenuse but different projection splits, showing how the shape changes while the sum stays fixed.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('hypotenuse-equal-vs-unequal.svg')} alt="Two right triangles with the same hypotenuse length 10: one with equal projections 5 and 5, the other with projections 2 and 8" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Same hypotenuse, different shapes: p = q = 5 vs p = 2, q = 8.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-compare -->
<section id="sec-compare" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Hypotenuse From Projections vs. Hypotenuse From Legs</h2>
  <p>Two routes lead to the hypotenuse. When the altitude's segments are known, use segment addition: <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} />. When the two sides around the right angle are known instead, use the Pythagorean theorem: <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Choose the projection formula when the problem gives the altitude's foot and its two segments; choose the leg formula when it gives the two perpendicular sides. In one consistent triangle, both routes produce the same c.</p>
  <p>A quick cross-check ties the two formulas together. From projections <span set:html={rich('[[p = 9]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 16]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} />. The legs are <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 20]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{15^2 + 20^2} = \\sqrt{625} = 25]]')} />. Both routes land on the same hypotenuse.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Known Values</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Formula</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Method</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Projections p and q</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">c = p + q</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Segment addition</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Legs a and b</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">c = √(a² + b²)</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Pythagorean theorem</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Hypotenuse From Projections</h2>
  <p>Each mistake below is followed by its correction.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Multiplying p and q instead of adding them. With <span set:html={rich('[[p = 4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9]]')} />, the product 36 is meaningless here; the hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[4 + 9 = 13]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Confusing projections with legs. The legs meet at the right angle; the projections lie along the hypotenuse. Label each length before calculating.</li>
    <li>Using the altitude as a projection. The altitude h is perpendicular to the hypotenuse, not a piece of it, so it never belongs in <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Mixing measurement units. <span set:html={rich('[[p = 40]]')} /> mm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9]]')} /> cm must become 4 cm and 9 cm before adding; the raw sum 49 has no valid unit.</li>
    <li>Entering negative lengths. Projections are positive by definition, so a negative entry signals a data error.</li>
    <li>Confusing the hypotenuse with a leg. The hypotenuse sits opposite the right angle and is the longest side; a leg can never play its role.</li>
    <li>Forgetting one segment. Adding only p gives a partial length; the hypotenuse needs both parts.</li>
    <li>Using unrelated measurements. The two numbers must be the adjacent parts of the same hypotenuse, not arbitrary lengths from the problem.</li>
    <li>Applying the Pythagorean theorem to p and q. The value <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{p^2 + q^2}]]')} /> is not the hypotenuse, because p and q are collinear, not perpendicular.</li>
    <li>Confusing <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{pq}]]')} />. Addition finds the hypotenuse; the geometric mean finds the altitude. Swapping the formulas swaps the answers.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If you catch yourself mid-mistake, stop and relabel the diagram before recalculating. Most of the errors above vanish once p and q are clearly marked on the hypotenuse and everything else is crossed out.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of Hypotenuse Projection Calculations</h2>
  <p>In classrooms and homework, projection problems test whether students can label a diagram and apply <span set:html={rich('[[c = p + q]]')} /> instead of reaching for the Pythagorean theorem. Technical drawing exercises use the same skill when an altitude splits a measured hypotenuse on a plan.</p>
  <p>Surveying models and architectural geometry sometimes reduce a right-triangle layout to a hypotenuse cut by a perpendicular, where adding the two marked segments gives the full span. Geometric constructions on paper use the sum to set compass widths.</p>
  <p>The condition is always the same: p and q must be adjacent parts of one hypotenuse in a genuine right triangle. Arbitrary distances never qualify, no matter how tempting the addition looks.</p>
  <p>Teachers can use the projection setup as a bridge lesson: it reviews segment addition from earlier grades while previewing the similarity theorems that arrive later in the geometry course.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Try these six problems, then check the worked answers below. None of the values repeat the worked examples.</p>
  <p>Work each problem on paper before reading its answer. Cover the answers with your hand if needed; the multi-step problem is the best test of whether the projection theorems have sunk in.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Whole Numbers</h3>
  <p>A right triangle's altitude divides the hypotenuse into <span set:html={rich('[[p = 12]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 4]]')} /> cm. Find c.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Decimals</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 2.5]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 7.5]]')} /> m, find the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Equal Projections</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 7]]')} /> in and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 7]]')} /> in, find c. What kind of triangle is it?</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Unit Conversion</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 200]]')} /> mm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 35]]')} /> cm, find the hypotenuse in centimeters.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Missing Projection</h3>
  <p>A hypotenuse measures <span set:html={rich('[[c = 50]]')} /> cm and one projection is <span set:html={rich('[[q = 32]]')} /> cm. Find p.</p>
  <h3>Problem 6: Multi-Step With Legs</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[p = 16]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9]]')} /> cm, find c, then find legs a and b.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>Answer 1: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 12 + 4 = 16]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 2: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2.5 + 7.5 = 10]]')} /> m.</p>
  <p>Answer 3: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7 + 7 = 14]]')} /> in. Equal projections give an isosceles right triangle.</p>
  <p>Answer 4: Convert 200 mm to 20 cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[c = 20 + 35 = 55]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 5: <span set:html={rich('[[p = c - q = 50 - 32 = 18]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 6: <span set:html={rich('[[c = 16 + 9 = 25]]')} /> cm. Then <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{25 \\times 16} = 20]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{25 \\times 9} = 15]]')} /> cm.</p>
</section>

